00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:31.176
MARK MANDEL: OK, cool.

00:05:31.176 --> 00:05:35.440
So Cloud Endpoints recently got
re-released, or released again,

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.340
or enhanced.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:38.030
It's different.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:38.560
It's new.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.150
Tell us what Cloud
Endpoints is now.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:41.310
What does it do?

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:44.580
DAN CIRULI: Endpoints is--
Cloud Endpoints is Google's API

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:45.850
management product.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:46.629
And you're right.

00:05:46.629 --> 00:05:47.920
There's a previous incarnation.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.586
I'm sure we'll get
into it a little bit.

00:05:49.586 --> 00:05:51.190
It used to be a
feature of App Engine.

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:54.305
And it's now a
product on its own.

00:05:54.305 --> 00:05:57.460
And Endpoints lets
you manage your APIs.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.305
And by manage, I mean you want
to get information from them,

00:06:00.305 --> 00:06:02.430
know who's using them, how
much they're using them.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:06.300
You want to be able to control,
authorize, things like that.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.220
Google Cloud Endpoints
is what lets customers

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:11.620
do that on Google Cloud.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So basically, it's

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.222
going to allow me to
provide REST APIs?

00:06:17.222 --> 00:06:19.180
DAN CIRULI: Well, there's
a lot of ways for you

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:20.530
to provide REST APIs.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:21.030
Right.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:21.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.900
DAN CIRULI: I don't know what
your favorite language is.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:24.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Python.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:26.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:27.970
DAN CIRULI: Let's
say it was Python.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:31.622
You might want to use Flask
or Django to make a REST API.

00:06:31.622 --> 00:06:34.080
You might like a language where
people don't use frameworks

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.120
so much to make APIs.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:36.750
And that's OK.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.180
What we want to
do with Endpoints

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:40.960
is control who has
access to that API

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.540
and give you really good
monitoring information about

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.150
who's using that API and how.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.270
Is it healthy?

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:48.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK,
so it is not much as

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:50.720
about serving an API
as much as giving

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.667
like a layer of protection
and authentication,

00:06:54.667 --> 00:06:55.500
and stuff like that.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.000
On top of the REST API.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.640
DAN CIRULI: Right,
and we do have

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:00.677
a couple of API frameworks.

00:07:00.677 --> 00:07:01.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:03.135
DAN CIRULI: One in
Java, one in Python.

00:07:03.135 --> 00:07:05.010
Those started its
App Engine features.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.970
They've now been open
sourced and can run anywhere.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:09.900
Those actually are
lightweight frameworks

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.620
that can help you serve an API.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.249
But most people already
have a favorite framework

00:07:15.249 --> 00:07:16.040
that they're using.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:16.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:18.371
DAN CIRULI: We already
have a language of choice.

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:20.100
We know that we want
to be able to work

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.460
with anybody, regardless
of language and framework.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:23.960
MARK MANDEL: So if
I got this right.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:25.800
So if I'm building
a REST Endpoint

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.300
and I want common features,
say like [? logging ?]

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:30.650
or authentication, or
some other things along

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.365
those sort of lines.

00:07:32.365 --> 00:07:34.240
That's the sort of stuff
that I'm looking at.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.160
So what then are the-- if you
had to give four or five bullet

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.750
points for that tooling that
I wanted with Endpoints,

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.400
is it [? logging? ?] You
mentioned authorization.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:43.414
What are those points?

00:07:43.414 --> 00:07:45.080
DAN CIRULI: So we did
a bunch of surveys

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:47.600
before we got going
with this to find out

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:48.800
what our customers needed.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.880
And people use APIs in a
big variety of situations.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:55.830
Sometimes it's to power
their mobile apps.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.507
Sometimes it's between
micro-services.

00:07:58.507 --> 00:08:00.340
Sometimes it's for their
partners to use it.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:02.131
But there is a certain
core set of features

00:08:02.131 --> 00:08:04.180
that everybody seemed to need.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:05.900
First of all is authentication.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:06.920
Right.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:08.880
Whether it's a service
that's making a call

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.470
or it's a user that's
holding your mobile app

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.011
or logged into your
website, you need

00:08:12.011 --> 00:08:15.780
to know on behalf of whom
that API call is being made.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:18.820
Second is logging
and monitoring.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:20.200
Ops are very important.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.830
Status is very important.

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:24.590
And you want to be able to
know is the API working?

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:27.780
Is it working well and
what are latencies?

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.330
Scalability always
comes in really huge.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.419
Even customers who aren't
running at large scale,

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:35.000
it actually comes in number
one on their long list

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:35.740
of requirements.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:37.650
They want to know
that, if it takes off,

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:41.820
if I become the next Pokemon Go,
if I even need 1,000 requests

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.679
a second, can this do it?

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:45.220
And then the last
thing is, they want

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:46.210
to know who's making the call?

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:47.668
Which developer is
making the call?

00:08:47.668 --> 00:08:50.000
Every API call actually
represents two entities, right?

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.890
There's the developer
who wrote that code.

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:53.000
You want to know that.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.850
And you want to
know the user who's

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:57.322
holding that mobile device.

00:08:57.322 --> 00:08:58.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:00.980
So could you talk a little
bit more about that.

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:02.810
So you talked about
authentication

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:07.020
and who is doing it.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:09.350
How do you explain that?

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:12.131
What are the ways I can do that?

00:09:12.131 --> 00:09:12.630
Right.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:17.130
So some of the ways that
Google's own APIs provide

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.820
authentication to
their consumers

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:24.280
are with Google's API
keys and OAuth tokens

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:27.830
and service-to-service auth.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:29.420
And all of those
ways are actually

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:32.670
available for Endpoints
service producers as well.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.950
So if you have your
own API and want

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:37.260
to put your own mobile
app in front of it,

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.690
you can use the same
authentication mechanisms

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:43.420
that you would use if
that mobile app was

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:45.324
calling one of Google's APIs.

00:09:45.324 --> 00:09:46.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:47.740
MARK MANDEL: Well, you
mentioned previously, Dan,

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:50.330
that there was a previous
version of Endpoints that

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:51.900
works with App Engine.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.560
So are there any
limitations technologically

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:56.352
in terms of how I can
do my rest endpoints,

00:09:56.352 --> 00:09:57.851
or is that just
like a free-for-all?

00:09:57.851 --> 00:09:58.930
Can I put it anywhere?

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:00.263
DAN CIRULI: It's a free-for-all.

00:10:00.263 --> 00:10:01.610
You can put it anywhere.

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.800
As you may or may not
know, we are members

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.730
and we're founding members
of the Open API Initiative.

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:09.990
And the Open API
Initiative is a bunch

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:12.150
of people, organizations
from around

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.620
the internet--
SmartBear, who owns

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:20.460
the Swagger Project, Apogee,
Microsoft, IBM, Capital One.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:22.990
A bunch of different providers
came together and said,

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:25.400
we should all agree
on a specification

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:27.180
format for an API.

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.487
And there's a bunch
of reasons for that,

00:10:29.487 --> 00:10:31.070
and maybe I'll get
into some of those.

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.653
But this is the spec, that used
to be called the Swagger spec,

00:10:33.653 --> 00:10:36.610
is now called the
Open API spec is how

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:38.352
you tell us about your APIs.

00:10:38.352 --> 00:10:39.810
So I don't care
how you develop it.

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.350
I don't care what
language you use.

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.410
I don't care what
framework you use.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:46.140
As long as you can give
endpoints, an Open API

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:49.610
specification for that
API, then Endpoints

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:52.480
can manage the API for you.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:53.427
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:10:53.427 --> 00:10:55.260
I'm going to redirect
to Sep, because you're

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:58.300
being really quiet over there.

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:01.270
But there are frameworks
that you said have come out.

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:03.350
There's a Java framework
and a Python framework.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:07.110
What does that give me, above
and beyond the tooling, that's

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:09.940
sort of like the infrastructure
tooling that I have available?

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:11.370
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: Yeah.

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:13.726
I want to turn your question
around a little bit.

00:11:13.726 --> 00:11:15.100
I think what you're
trying to ask

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:18.680
is, why should I use Endpoints?

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:20.950
These solutions and the
problems that we talked about

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:24.660
are not new, and there
are many other products

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.980
in this space that provide
logging and monitoring

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:28.280
and authentication.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:30.720
Why should I do Endpoints?

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:34.620
And we talked about
the customer who

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:36.830
suddenly has a lot of
success with their app

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:38.320
and has a lot of users.

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:42.650
Well, it turns out that need
for logging and monitoring

00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:45.280
and authentication
happens very quickly.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.820
As soon as you get a couple
hundred, a few thousand users,

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:50.420
it turns out that
your users will

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:52.860
care a lot about whether
your app is up and running

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.732
or not or whether your APIs
are up and running or not.

00:11:55.732 --> 00:11:57.440
And at that point,
it's a little too late

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:01.550
to go spend a couple of weeks
learning about a logging

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:04.000
service and a monitoring
service and then

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.580
a separate
authentication framework.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:10.080
What Endpoints does is it
packages all of those API

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:13.910
management features
into one solution,

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:16.960
and it tries to reduce
the amount of work

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:18.650
that the developer
has to do in order

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:21.120
to integrate with each
one of these features one

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:23.540
by one, by aggregating
all of them

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:27.670
and say, you, focus on
your core competency,

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.540
pick any framework you like,
pick any language you like,

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:33.710
and build your back end APIs.

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:36.250
What we will do is
we will provide you

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:39.300
all of these
features, and all you

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:41.800
have to do in order to
take advantage of them

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:45.020
is provide an Open API
specification for your API,

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:45.690
right?

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:48.620
So what is it that you get from
the frameworks specifically?

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:52.840
The frameworks allow you
to annotate your code

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:56.390
and generate that API
specification from it.

00:12:56.390 --> 00:13:02.200
And then you can choose to use
the API management features

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:04.760
or just use that Open
API specification

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:08.200
to generate clients in
different languages that

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:11.610
can call your API or take
advantage of other features,

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:16.270
like [INAUDIBLE] UIs that
let you explore your API.

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:17.570
Does that answer your question?

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:18.820
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I think so.

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:21.520
It sounds like basically the
frameworks make it easier

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:23.454
to come up with that
Swagger or Open API specs

00:13:23.454 --> 00:13:25.620
rather than trying to write
it by hand, for example.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:26.810
Would that be correct?

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:27.851
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: Yes.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:28.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.740
So the way you
explained it, I think

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.406
it's very interesting,
because it really

00:13:31.406 --> 00:13:34.440
sounds like there's basically
a bundle of features that

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:37.790
are opinionated, and it
makes your life easier

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:38.950
as a developer.

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:42.040
It really sounds like
App Engine to me.

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:45.865
And App Engine also comes
with some constraints, right?

00:13:45.865 --> 00:13:47.740
What are the constraints,
other than the fact

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:51.110
that you need to use an
Open API or a Swagger spec

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:52.090
for Endpoints?

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:53.930
Is there anything else
that we should know?

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:56.500
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: I will
redirect that to Dan, I think.

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:59.210
DAN CIRULI: Yeah,
no, I don't think so.

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:01.950
In some ways our
frameworks are opinionated.

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:05.600
But I would say that the
use of the Open API spec

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:09.159
is, in a sense, to
avoid "opinionation,"

00:14:09.159 --> 00:14:09.950
if that's the word.

00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:10.652
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:10.652 --> 00:14:12.610
We want you to be able
to choose your back end.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:14.710
And by, the way,
Cloud Endpoints-- I'm

00:14:14.710 --> 00:14:17.600
surprised we got this far in the
podcast before I mentioned it,

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:20.620
bad product manager--
runs on App Engine.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:23.010
But you can also use
it from Compute Engine.

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:25.190
You can use it from
a Container Engine.

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:27.250
You can use it from wherever.

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:30.630
The proxy that performs
the API management features

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:33.760
is designed to be containerized
and deployed anywhere.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:38.005
So one of the big requests
we had from existing people

00:14:38.005 --> 00:14:39.380
who were using
our frameworks was

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:40.700
they wanted to
move to App Engine

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:42.075
flexible environments
they wanted

00:14:42.075 --> 00:14:43.816
to move to Container Engine.

00:14:43.816 --> 00:14:44.690
Now they can do that.

00:14:45.055 --> 00:14:46.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:51.280
So could it work also on App
Engine with other frameworks

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:52.614
other than Java and Python?

00:14:52.614 --> 00:14:54.280
DAN CIRULI: That's a
fantastic question,

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:57.379
and the answer is not yet,
but we're working on that.

00:14:57.379 --> 00:14:59.170
We need some work from
the App Engine team.

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:01.295
We need some work from our
networking team as well.

00:15:01.295 --> 00:15:03.160
But we would love
you to be able to,

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:06.410
yeah, pick your own
favorite framework

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:07.860
and run that on App Engine.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.000
That's going to take
a coordinated effort,

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:11.530
but we'd love for
that to happen.

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:12.610
MARK MANDEL: Is that
a restriction that's

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:14.930
specific to App Engine
Standard or App Engine Standard

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:15.950
and Flexible?

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:17.280
DAN CIRULI: Flexible-- you
can do anything in Flexible.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:18.490
MARK MANDEL: So Flexible's fine.

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:20.573
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So it is
just like, for instance,

00:15:20.573 --> 00:15:22.560
if you have an App
Engine app running

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:26.720
Go, on Standard environment,
for now, it could not work.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:29.020
But you could use Flexible,
and it could work.

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:30.130
DAN CIRULI: That's
absolutely right.

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:32.680
In fact, that's why they call it
Flexible because it's, indeed--

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:33.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It's more flexible.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:35.250
That is actually
[INAUDIBLE] naming there.

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:36.760
DAN CIRULI: Among other reasons,
that's a little known fact.

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:38.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great
naming right there.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:40.820
MARK MANDEL: So we've talked
a lot about REST APIs here.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.290
But as many people who
listened to the podcast know,

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:45.280
we're huge fans of GRPC.

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:47.820
Can I use this with GRPC, Sep?

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:49.932
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH:
Yes, you can, actually.

00:15:49.932 --> 00:15:53.310
Our GRPC support is
coming very shortly.

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:55.770
It is currently in
the alpha stages.

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:59.670
And our customers can
sign up to use that.

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:03.460
And there are advantages
to using GRPC,

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:05.950
and there are advantages
to using JSON REST.

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:09.060
So you'll have to see
which one is the right fit

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.440
for your application.

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:13.160
DAN CIRULI: I was actually
really pleasantly surprised

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.230
at how quickly we
got that question

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:19.950
from our alpha participants and
how readily they adopted it.

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.810
It's sometimes a little
bit hard to gauge

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:24.870
the success of an open source
project, GRPC in particular.

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:28.130
We don't know how many
people are using GRPC.

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:29.510
It's more than I had thought.

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:32.020
And, yeah, we want
that same feature

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:34.687
set, because whether your
API's talking REST or not,

00:16:34.687 --> 00:16:36.270
you still want logging
and monitoring.

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:37.680
You still need authentication.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:38.830
You still need API keys.

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:41.270
So you want that same
dashboard regardless of what

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:42.927
that protocol is, right?

00:16:42.927 --> 00:16:44.260
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:45.593
Now, I'm actually quite curious.

00:16:45.593 --> 00:16:49.640
Since GRPC is kind of HTP2
binary spec, like Protos coming

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:51.650
in and out, do you
get any introspection

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:54.140
into like the data going in
and out or anything like that?

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:55.737
Do you get any cool
tools that way?

00:16:55.737 --> 00:16:57.070
DAN CIRULI: You don't right now.

00:16:57.070 --> 00:17:01.300
In fact, in either, whether
you're using REST JSON or GRPC,

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:03.210
we're not introspecting
your payload.

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:05.890
So we're not looking at
that payload in either case.

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:08.680
But we can tell you what
percentage of your calls

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:12.069
are errors per method, what your
latencies are like at median,

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:18.960
again, per method, which
client is making the call.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:21.349
And maybe you realize,
hey, our errors went up,

00:17:21.349 --> 00:17:23.020
but it's all in a
particular method,

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:25.290
and it's all from
only the iOS client.

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:27.040
So it lets you tell
very quickly, OK, this

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:29.935
is probably a client problem
with the new release we did.

00:17:29.935 --> 00:17:31.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
how do you manage

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:35.080
exposing a Swagger
or an Open API spec

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:38.020
when what you are using is GRPC?

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:38.860
How does that work?

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:42.720
GRPC has a design language.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:45.360
DAN CIRULI: Do you
want to take that, Sep?

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:46.650
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: Sure.

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:51.680
So our GRPC customers
inside and outside of Google

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:57.330
are currently using Proto to
define their API specification.

00:17:57.330 --> 00:18:00.630
We're currently
working on integration

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:06.970
with Open API for GRPC services,
using cyber extensions,

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:09.160
for example, and other options.

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:14.830
But internally, what we do is we
convert the API specifications

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:16.960
to the same common format.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:20.710
And the APIs we use
for that conversion

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:23.430
and exposing the
API specification

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:25.180
have also been published.

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:27.300
So if you're curious, you
can take a look at them,

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:34.170
and these would be under our
Service Management API set.

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.310
DAN CIRULI: Yeah,
and we can also

00:18:36.310 --> 00:18:38.730
make a-- we'll put in
the Resources section

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:40.420
a link to the GitHub project.

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:43.230
So we have this thing called
the Google API Compiler that

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:48.540
can take an Open API
specification or a Proto

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:50.650
and a Yaml, that
together describe

00:18:50.650 --> 00:18:52.586
how to manage a GRPC API.

00:18:52.586 --> 00:18:54.960
They compile them this thing
we call a normalized service

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:57.320
config, and that's actually
the format that all

00:18:57.320 --> 00:18:59.620
our downstream tools take.

00:18:59.620 --> 00:19:03.640
So one thing we would like to
see, as members of the Open API

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:07.440
Initiative we are
working with, could there

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:10.740
be a single canonical
representation of an API,

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:14.870
regardless of whether
it's HTP JSON and HTP 1.1

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:17.570
or Proto and HTP 2.

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:21.964
So as we work with that
committee, the Open API

00:19:21.964 --> 00:19:24.130
technical committee, we'd
like to see if there could

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:25.310
be a single representation.

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:27.977
But for now, it's either an Open
API spec or a Proto and a Yaml.

00:19:27.977 --> 00:19:29.309
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:19:29.309 --> 00:19:30.770
Now I'm just
thinking about how we

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:33.470
were talking about the GRPC
gateway that'll switch a GRPC

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:35.170
call into an HTP call.

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:37.730
So then you can put
Endpoints on top of both,

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:39.029
and that's pretty powerful.

00:19:39.029 --> 00:19:41.320
DAN CIRULI: Right, and then
certainly, we're doing that

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:42.680
internally at Google.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:45.950
A lot of our APIs now are
serving both the GRPC circuits

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:48.020
as well as a JSON surface.

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:49.906
And we'll have that, for sure.

00:19:49.906 --> 00:19:53.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, we had
Brandon [? Philips ?] last week

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:55.940
talking about how to
use GRPC [INAUDIBLE],

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:57.350
and they also
mentioned that they

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:00.864
were using the GRPC gateway,
which was very nice to hear.

00:20:00.864 --> 00:20:02.280
DAN CIRULI: Obviously
I know that,

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:03.446
because I listen every week.

00:20:03.785 --> 00:20:05.076
FRANCESC CAMPOY: As you should.

00:20:05.076 --> 00:20:05.750
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:06.990
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: Yeah, the
nice thing, as you mentioned,

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:10.060
is that you write your
back end, and it typically

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:12.150
handles either GRPC or REST.

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:16.470
But once you use Endpoints and
have that extensible server

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.470
proxy in front of
your back end, you

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:20.290
can transport back and forth.

00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:23.230
And so your clients are
free to use whichever

00:20:23.230 --> 00:20:25.920
suits their needs better.

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:29.680
DAN CIRULI: And we do want
to work with the standards

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:33.010
committee on making sure that
it's a canonical transformation

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:35.500
that you are
describing completely

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:37.630
with either one of those
documents what your API

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:39.255
surface is.

00:20:39.255 --> 00:20:39.880
FRANCESC: Cool.

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:44.090
I had a question regarding one
of my least favorite topics,

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:46.080
which is OAuth 2.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:48.360
What other kind of
authentications do you have?

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:50.760
You talked about one
of my favorite ones,

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:54.780
which is API keys, because
they're super easy to use.

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:56.730
Do you have any other options?

00:20:56.730 --> 00:21:00.260
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: Yeah, so we
do support Gitkit, or Firebase

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:02.940
Auth, as it's called now.

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:07.060
We support other third-party
authentication providers,

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:09.350
like Auth 0.

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:10.930
Am I missing any others, Dan?

00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:13.138
DAN CIRULI: No, I think
those are the important ones.

00:21:13.138 --> 00:21:16.050
And both those, Firebase
Auth and Auth 0,

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:18.170
are used by mobile
developers, especially

00:21:18.170 --> 00:21:20.580
who want their users
to log in, say,

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:24.460
with Facebook or with username
and password or your Twitter

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:25.590
login or your GitHub login.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:26.150
It doesn't matter.

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:27.680
They have integrations
with those.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:31.450
So your end user, maybe they
just log in with Facebook,

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:34.770
both those solutions will
generate a token, a JSON web

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:36.480
token that is passed across.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.960
Endpoint can validate it
and say, yes, this really

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:41.350
was generated by your client.

00:21:41.350 --> 00:21:42.280
It's an auth token.

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:44.560
It's valid, and here's
your user ID out of it.

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:45.830
MARK MANDEL: So you've
got options there.

00:21:45.830 --> 00:21:47.390
So if you've got something
like mobile clients

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:49.310
and you want that particular
user to be logging in,

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:51.620
and then also if you're doing
maybe like service to service,

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:52.150
you've got those options, too.

00:21:52.150 --> 00:21:52.840
DAN CIRULI: Right.

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:54.506
And that's one of the
interesting things

00:21:54.506 --> 00:21:56.420
we saw that many
of our customers

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:58.370
are actually doing
two types of auth.

00:21:58.370 --> 00:22:00.330
They've got an
external-facing service

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:03.420
that is validating a
user-generated auth token,

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.320
say the user logged
in with Facebook.

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:07.460
But then that's, in turn,
calling micro services.

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:09.710
And so they're using Endpoints
on those micro services

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:11.940
as well, and those are
taking service to service.

00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:14.120
So they're validating
an auth token

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:16.120
that was created in one
of their other services.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:17.572
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:22:17.572 --> 00:22:21.070
At least from the point
of view of the client

00:22:21.070 --> 00:22:23.750
calling the server, having
things like Firebase Auth

00:22:23.750 --> 00:22:25.880
is so much better,
just because you

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:30.450
don't need to handle the
OAuth 2 dance by yourself,

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:31.590
and that's a huge thing.

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:33.520
I don't know a
single developer that

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:35.790
is happy to write that
part of the code, ever.

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:36.050
DAN CIRULI: Right.

00:22:36.050 --> 00:22:37.050
No one wants to do that.

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:38.972
That's right.

00:22:38.972 --> 00:22:40.680
MARK MANDEL: So both
of you have actually

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:44.020
mentioned this thing called
an extensible server proxy,

00:22:44.020 --> 00:22:46.040
or something along
those sort of lines.

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:47.940
Does one of you want to
tell us what that is,

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:49.980
and why is it extensible,
and why is it a proxy?

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:51.570
Like, what does it do?

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:54.950
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: (LAUGHING)
Sure, I can tackle that.

00:22:54.950 --> 00:22:57.560
So as I mentioned, we
want the developers

00:22:57.560 --> 00:23:00.180
to be free to choose any
language, any framework

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:05.470
that they want, and build
and host their APIs anywhere.

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:08.980
So what we're providing is
this extensible server proxy,

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:13.620
which is an Nginx-based
web server that sits

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:15.820
in front of their applications.

00:23:15.820 --> 00:23:17.680
It's extensible
because currently it

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:19.610
provides features
like authentication,

00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:21.370
monitoring, and logging.

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:24.200
In the future, we will
be adding other features

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:28.520
that our API management
customers are looking for.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:31.600
And it's also extensible in
the sense that, as you know,

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:34.750
Nginx is an open
source project, and we

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:37.640
are working towards
open sourcing the proxy

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:40.980
as well in order to
have the open source

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:45.220
community be able to take it
and add other features that they

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:46.610
want.

00:23:46.610 --> 00:23:49.010
DAN CIRULI: One interesting
thing about the way Endpoints

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:51.385
is architected-- it's different
than virtually everybody,

00:23:51.385 --> 00:23:54.050
certainly every other cloud
provider's solution-- is

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:57.090
that our proxy is designed
to be containerized.

00:23:57.090 --> 00:24:00.370
It's not what we'd think of
as a fat-shared proxy, where

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:02.996
every customer's API calls
are going through the same one

00:24:02.996 --> 00:24:04.620
and then being routed
to your back end.

00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:08.280
Rather this is a thin
container that gets deployed

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:11.110
alongside with your back end.

00:24:11.110 --> 00:24:14.210
Internally at Google, we made
a switch from a shared proxy

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:17.820
to what we call a distributed
or server local proxy about two

00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:19.220
years ago.

00:24:19.220 --> 00:24:21.660
We did it for
performance reasons.

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:24.220
It reduces by an
order of magnitude

00:24:24.220 --> 00:24:27.330
the amount of latency
introduced at the proxy.

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:29.930
It reduces a network hop.

00:24:29.930 --> 00:24:31.917
And it's much more scalable.

00:24:31.917 --> 00:24:33.000
So for all those reasons--

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:33.720
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: Yeah,
the other nice thing about it

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:34.790
is the decoupling.

00:24:34.790 --> 00:24:38.060
If you have multiple APIs or
multiple micro services talking

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:41.310
to each other, you
can update the proxy

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:44.270
sitting in front of one of them
without affecting the others.

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:46.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
a very good point.

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:48.140
So just make sure
I'm understanding

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:52.980
this, that means that rather
than having that exposing cloud

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:56.900
endpoint IP, where people go
always there and then be handed

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:59.200
there maybe a load
balancer and then

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:01.760
be handed a bunch of
machines, what you're saying

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:04.170
is that you could
still have basically

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:06.330
the same architecture,
where you're exposing

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:08.220
an IP from your load balancer.

00:25:08.220 --> 00:25:10.730
And then when it gets
to every single machine,

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:12.189
those machines are
running a proxy.

00:25:12.189 --> 00:25:13.229
DAN CIRULI: That's right.

00:25:13.229 --> 00:25:14.867
Think of it as just
another container

00:25:14.867 --> 00:25:15.950
running on those machines.

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:19.350
And if you're using, say,
GKE or App Engine Flex,

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:21.120
we make it trivial.

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:23.770
With App Engine Flex, that
container just pops up.

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:24.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, it's just there.

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:26.578
DAN CIRULI: We see that there's
an API as part of your app,

00:25:26.578 --> 00:25:27.510
and so we spin it up.

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:30.827
We wire traffic through it.

00:25:30.827 --> 00:25:32.660
And so you really don't
have to do anything.

00:25:32.660 --> 00:25:36.670
If you're using GKE, you just
say, throw this container here.

00:25:36.670 --> 00:25:39.710
And as your app
scales and, say, you

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:43.010
go from serving 10 requests a
serving 1,000 requests a second

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:45.940
and you scale up that back
end, each one that comes up,

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:47.550
a server local proxy
comes up with it.

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:48.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:48.470
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: Right.

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:50.719
I just want to clarify,
though, that even though we're

00:25:50.719 --> 00:25:53.620
big fans of containers and
use them widely within Google

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:55.910
and we're big fans of
the Docker Project,

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:58.200
you don't actually
have to use containers.

00:25:58.200 --> 00:25:59.660
They make your
life a lot easier.

00:25:59.660 --> 00:26:02.370
But if you want to
just deploy your code

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:07.460
on a raw GCEVM or a virtual
machine somewhere else,

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:08.890
you can definitely do that, too.

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:10.290
DAN CIRULI: I like that--
they make your life easier.

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:12.123
I mean, if you like
pain, sure, we got pain.

00:26:12.123 --> 00:26:12.817
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:12.817 --> 00:26:14.650
MARK MANDEL: So Sep,
you said something kind

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:16.233
of really interesting,
but you kind of

00:26:16.233 --> 00:26:19.312
glanced past it, which is you
said the proxy's open source?

00:26:19.312 --> 00:26:20.770
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH:
Nginx Project,

00:26:20.770 --> 00:26:23.160
which the proxy is based
on, is open source.

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:25.310
So we're working on
open sourcing this.

00:26:25.310 --> 00:26:27.350
But there are a lot of
challenges open sourcing

00:26:27.350 --> 00:26:30.620
a very large project.

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:33.340
DAN CIRULI: And I would say
that the challenges were there

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:35.710
some teams we're aligning
with internally at Google.

00:26:35.710 --> 00:26:38.520
This is a very interesting
area-- as well as outside.

00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:43.110
One of the reasons that projects
like GRPC and Kubernetes

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:45.990
have done very well is that
we actually got partners

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:47.770
in there and working
with the code

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:49.790
before it went to open
source, and it makes

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:51.260
sure you're in a certain state.

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:53.617
And so we're kind of in
that process right now.

00:26:53.617 --> 00:26:55.950
There's a lot of people very
interested in this project,

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:57.533
and we want to make
sure that we don't

00:26:57.533 --> 00:27:00.095
have to make too big
of changes after going

00:27:00.095 --> 00:27:00.970
[INAUDIBLE] with it.

00:27:00.970 --> 00:27:02.440
So we hope to do something soon.

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:03.690
MARK MANDEL: All right, sweet.

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:04.815
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH: Right.

00:27:04.815 --> 00:27:08.270
And as Dan mentioned, our
tooling and the implementation

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:10.590
of the APIs that I mentioned
are basically open source

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:11.880
right now.

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:16.240
It's very important to us to not
lock our customers in, to say,

00:27:16.240 --> 00:27:19.270
you have to use Google Cloud
or you have to use Endpoints.

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:20.850
We would like these
components to be

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:23.530
able to stand alone
and for the APIs that

00:27:23.530 --> 00:27:25.660
expose the infrastructure,
which is really

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:26.990
doing the heavy lifting.

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:30.490
It's the same infrastructure
that's running Google's APIs.

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:33.460
You could leverage
it to host your APIs

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:36.620
and take advantage of the
same API management features.

00:27:36.620 --> 00:27:39.570
So that's what we are
really putting out there,

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:43.100
and we would like people to be
able to look at the proxy code,

00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:47.480
look at the tooling code that
converts and analyzes the Open

00:27:47.480 --> 00:27:50.620
API specification and also to
be able to extend it, maybe

00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:52.014
contribute features.

00:27:52.014 --> 00:27:53.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So does this mean

00:27:53.430 --> 00:27:57.480
that since we can run
Endpoints basically anywhere,

00:27:57.480 --> 00:28:01.850
I could run the back end for
Google Cloud Endpoints outside

00:28:01.850 --> 00:28:03.120
of the Google Cloud?

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:04.470
DAN CIRULI: Yes.

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:06.020
SEPEHR EBRAHIMZADEH:
That is correct.

00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:08.740
We are not providing
documentation and support

00:28:08.740 --> 00:28:11.904
for doing so yet, but
it is work in progress.

00:28:11.904 --> 00:28:12.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:14.540
I think that can be very
interesting, especially

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:17.010
for people considering
multi-cloud, the fact that you

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:20.334
can, with Stackdriver, you can
have monitoring multi-cloud.

00:28:20.334 --> 00:28:22.250
And now with Cloud
Endpoints, you can actually

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:23.710
have also that in multi-cloud.

00:28:23.710 --> 00:28:24.630
That is very cool.

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:26.590
MARK MANDEL: Very cool indeed.

