00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:50.370
The first one's from
[? Rashandovorani? ?]

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:52.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I would
say so-- [? Roshandarani. ?]

00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:54.855
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, OK, cool.

00:31:54.855 --> 00:31:56.310
Hi, Rashan.

00:31:56.310 --> 00:32:01.450
He has a question about project
level custom data, or metadata,

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:05.010
being able to access
it from App Engine.

00:32:05.010 --> 00:32:06.510
he doesn't seem to
be able to do it.

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:08.200
And he was like, hey,
Franscesc, can you

00:32:08.200 --> 00:32:10.283
help me, because this
doesn't seem to work for me?

00:32:10.283 --> 00:32:12.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah, we
answered a question regarding

00:32:12.960 --> 00:32:17.290
where to store information
like variables, environment

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:21.200
variables and stuff like
that, a couple episodes ago.

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:23.000
And we mentioned that
one of the places

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:25.990
was the metadata service that
has the benefit that you can

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:28.570
actually access it from
both Compute Engine

00:32:28.570 --> 00:32:31.780
but also a Container
Engine and even App Engine.

00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:34.060
And so to use it
from a App Engine,

00:32:34.060 --> 00:32:37.620
though, the important thing
is that rather than targeting

00:32:37.620 --> 00:32:42.470
the metadata.Google.internal
URL, that is indeed internal

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:44.850
and it is not accessible
from App Engine

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:46.450
unfortunately,
what you need to do

00:32:46.450 --> 00:32:50.030
is you need to use
the Compute API.

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:51.740
So the Compute
API is a REST API,

00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:53.950
and you can use it
from anywhere, as long

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:56.176
as you have the authentication.

00:32:56.176 --> 00:32:58.050
And the default
authentication for App Engine

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:03.367
provides that, as long as you
ask for the compute scope.

00:33:03.367 --> 00:33:04.950
And basically what
you're going to ask

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:07.440
is you going to ask for the
information about the project.

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:08.820
It is not a metadata thing.

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:10.890
It is actually inside
of the project resource.

00:33:10.890 --> 00:33:12.660
You're going to have
a bunch of things.

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:15.500
And of them is all
the metadata variables

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:18.060
attached to that project,
not only to the instance.

00:33:18.060 --> 00:33:20.530
So it is something that
you can definitely do.

00:33:20.530 --> 00:33:22.780
It may be a little bit
confusing at the beginning

00:33:22.780 --> 00:33:24.363
if you don't know
where to look at it.

00:33:24.363 --> 00:33:27.030
So I think it was a question
that was worth answering here.

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:28.130
MARK MANDEL: So
basically, rather than

00:33:28.130 --> 00:33:29.350
trying to access it
like you're running

00:33:29.350 --> 00:33:31.290
on Compute Engine,
access it like you're

00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:33.150
an external service coming in.

00:33:33.150 --> 00:33:34.525
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, basically,

00:33:34.525 --> 00:33:36.280
like if it was a
mobile app or something

00:33:36.280 --> 00:33:39.492
like that, that's how you could
access it exactly the same way.

00:33:39.492 --> 00:33:41.450
The good thing is you
don't need to really care

00:33:41.450 --> 00:33:44.020
about authentication because
the default authentication

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:46.104
from App Engine, it is
good enough to access that.

00:33:46.104 --> 00:33:46.853
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:33:46.853 --> 00:33:47.760
That makes sense.

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:48.380
Cool.

00:33:48.380 --> 00:33:49.838
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And then, yeah, we

00:33:49.838 --> 00:33:53.570
had another question from
Leonard Benoit or Bennett.

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:54.169
I don't know.

00:33:54.169 --> 00:33:55.210
MARK MANDEL: Sounds good.

