00:34:03.870 --> 00:34:08.690
So Leonard asks, OK, so we
love Google Cloud Platform.

00:34:08.690 --> 00:34:11.739
That is good. [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:11.739 --> 00:34:13.449
And they have an
application, which

00:34:13.449 --> 00:34:15.699
they're running the back end
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:34:15.699 --> 00:34:17.420
But then they have
front ends which

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:20.980
are an Android app, an iOS
app, and then our browser web

00:34:20.980 --> 00:34:22.100
interface.

00:34:22.100 --> 00:34:23.909
And the problem is
like, OK, we don't

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:27.040
want to write the code
for those applications

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:28.900
over and over for
every single time

00:34:28.900 --> 00:34:32.190
we have a little change have
to update all our clients.

00:34:32.190 --> 00:34:38.310
So how can we make this better
to reuse more code, basically?

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:40.243
And I think that you
may know about this.

00:34:40.243 --> 00:34:42.659
MARK MANDEL: So we might have
talked about it a little bit

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:45.360
today, which I think
is really cool.

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:48.270
So, yeah, if you want to
look at Cloud Endpoints,

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:50.719
I think that's a really
good way to go, particularly

00:34:50.719 --> 00:34:54.842
because it conforms to the
Swagger or the API spec.

00:34:54.842 --> 00:34:56.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
the Open API spec, yeah.

00:34:56.800 --> 00:34:58.758
MARK MANDEL: And what's
cool about that is then

00:34:58.758 --> 00:35:00.620
you can generate clients
for it, and that's

00:35:00.620 --> 00:35:02.703
where I think you're going
to get a lot of benefit

00:35:02.703 --> 00:35:03.230
from things.

00:35:03.230 --> 00:35:06.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that is actually basically

00:35:06.260 --> 00:35:10.310
what Google Cloud Endpoints used
to do for App Engine for Java

00:35:10.310 --> 00:35:10.810
and Python.

00:35:10.810 --> 00:35:12.309
That was exactly
what it did, right?

00:35:12.309 --> 00:35:16.920
Like, it could generate
some specification,

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:18.352
which was not Open API spec.

00:35:18.352 --> 00:35:19.310
It was a different one.

00:35:19.310 --> 00:35:21.090
But from there, you
can autogenerate

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:25.300
JavaScript code, Objective
C code, and Java code.

00:35:25.300 --> 00:35:27.640
With Swagger or
Open API Spec, you

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:30.950
can do the same thing, but with
many other languages, which

00:35:30.950 --> 00:35:32.300
is pretty amazing.

00:35:32.300 --> 00:35:33.065
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:35:33.065 --> 00:35:35.440
I'd be interested-- depending
on what it is you're doing,

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:37.240
you could also go
down the GRPC route.

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:39.800
But you may have some troubles
